course: dictionary
lesson: 10158
title: Thunder: noun and verb
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Thunder» is a word you can use as a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action). In «Thunder is the sound of air suddenly heated by lightning», it names the loud sound. In «It thundered all night», it tells you what the sky did. Look at the words around it to see which job it does.
explain: The context also tells you what the word means. «The train thundered past» does not mean the sky made a sound. It means the train moved past very loudly and fast. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The train made a loud rumbling sound past" still makes some sense, so you can trust your guess.
explain: Science explains the sound. Lightning heats the air very fast, and the air expands with a bang. That bang is thunder. Light travels much faster than sound, so «we see lightning before we hear thunder.» The gap between the flash and the bang tells you how far away the storm is.
explain: Words can also tell you what to do. «If you hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning, so you go indoors.» When you read a safety sentence, the word thunder is a warning, not just a sound.
fact: Thunder is the loud rumbling or crashing sound in the sky that comes after lightning.
fact: «Thunder» can be a noun («a clap of thunder») or a verb («It thundered all night»).
fact: «Thunder» is the sound of air suddenly heated by lightning and expanding very fast.
fact: Light travels faster than sound, so you see lightning before you hear thunder.
fact: Counting the seconds between the flash and the bang tells you how far away the storm is: about 3 seconds means about 1 km.
exercise: «It thundered all night.» What does thundered mean here? | It was very hot and still all night | It glowed like a light | *It made a loud rumbling sound all night | It rained softly all night
exercise: «The train thundered past.» What does thundered mean here? | Fell over slowly | *Moved past very loudly | Slept beside the track | Stopped quietly at the station
exercise: «Light travels much faster than sound, so we see lightning before we hear thunder.» Why do we see the lightning first? | Sound is too heavy to fly | Thunder is made before the lightning | Lightning is louder than thunder | *Light travels faster than sound
test: «If you hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck by lightning, so you go indoors.» What should you do when you hear thunder? | *Go indoors | Shout back at it | Stay under a tree | Count to a hundred outside
test: The sound of thunder travels about one kilometre in three seconds. If you count 6 seconds between the flash and the bang, how far away is the storm? | *2 km | 6 km | 1 km | 10 km
exam: «The thunder of hooves filled the field.» What does thunder mean in this sentence? | A quiet whisper | A kind of horse | *A very loud noise | A wet, grey day
